#{{AIHEADER}}:1
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A WORKED WAYSTONE EXAMPLE — COPY THIS, DO NOT LOAD IT.
#
# This file is NOT a card. It is deliberately named `waystone_EXAMPLE`, not
# `_waystone`, so no gate reads it, no coverage counter scores it, and nothing
# breaks when the fictional paths below do not exist. It ships with the template
# on purpose: START_HERE §WISL waystones tells you HOW to author a card, and the
# schema lives at the workspace canonical — but a stamped repo may sit on a
# machine that cannot reach the workspace, and instructions with no worked
# example in hand are how a convention quietly stops being followed.
#
# HOW TO USE IT: when a nameable seam emerges, copy the block between the two
# frontmatter fences into `<that folder>/_waystone.{{AI}}ai`, delete every `#` comment, and
# replace all of it with what is actually true. A card carried over with any of
# this text still in it is worse than no card, because it will be believed.
#
# THE ONE RULE THAT MATTERS MOST: say what `validation:` actually proves AND what
# it does not. "Scaffold only, nothing runs yet" is a correct `state`. A
# directory-existence check must never read as a passing test suite.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
---
wisl_version: 1                    # schema version; tools reject an unknown major
folder: src/ingest                 # MUST equal this card's own location
orient: "Ingest — parses inbound feed files into normalized records and hands them to src/store. ONE JOB: everything about the wire format lives here and nothing about the wire format lives anywhere else. Deliberately NOT `owns: **` at a repo root: a blanket claim scores as full coverage while leaving every subsystem unrouted, which is how a repo reads as 100% covered and routes nothing."
owns:                              # GATE-CRITICAL. Repo-relative globs this folder is authoritative for.
  - src/ingest/**                  # One glob per line, two-space indent — the bash extractor depends on it.
depends_on:                        # Orientation edges only — a folder edge pulls the WHOLE folder.
  - src/store                      # where parsed records go; NOT a file-set derivation, that is boot_path
risk: >-
  What is dangerous or high-judgment HERE, in specifics a newcomer could not guess.
  Vague risk text is the most commonly skipped field and the most expensive one to
  omit. Name the trap, not the category: "the parser accepts a truncated final
  record and silently drops it" beats "be careful with parsing".
continuity: "Where this folder's thread lives — e.g. readme_AI THR-004. Points at the story, not the code."
validation: "make test-ingest   # unit tests over the parser; does NOT exercise a real feed"
reviewed_at: "0000000"             # QUOTED git sha, 7-40 hex. A HUMAN reconciled `owns` with reality at
                                   # this commit. Re-stamp it in the SAME COMMIT as any change to an
                                   # owned file, or waystone-freshness fails — that is the gate working.
actors:                            # perm is read | write | none
  cloud: write
  chloe-local: read                # source folders are `read`: the local model never self-edits its source
  codex: read
boot_path:                         # The bounded, ORDERED read-set. This is where WISL's value actually is.
  - src/ingest/parser.py           # Order small-first: a small entry passes its surplus forward to the
  - src/ingest/schema.py           # next, so a big file later in the list gets a larger allocation.
# `heywy` is OPTIONAL and MUST BE THE LAST FRONTMATTER KEY. Every bash list
# extractor on the gate path reads indented lines until the next top-level key,
# so anything placed after `heywy` gets swallowed by its scan.
#
# NOTE WHAT JUST HAPPENED HERE: these lines are comments because they sit OUTSIDE
# any value. Inside a `>-` block scalar below, a `#` is ordinary text — it does
# NOT start a comment, and it will end up in the value verbatim. That trap is
# easy to hit and invisible until something renders the card.
heywy:
  what: >-
    Plain language, for {{HUMAN}}, not for an agent. What this is, in the terms
    someone would use if they did not know the codebase.
  state: >-
    What actually works today, and what only looks like it does. This is the field
    that earns trust; overstate here once and the whole card stops being read.
  run: >-
    The command to make it go.
  check: >-
    How to tell it is right — AND what a green result does not prove.
  next: >-
    The honest next step.
---
Prose orientation goes below the closing fence. Free text, read by humans and
agents alike. Say what to read first and in what order, where the real depth is
(folder count is a terrible proxy for complexity), and the gotchas that cost
somebody an afternoon.

Keep it short enough to survive the per-entry boot ceiling. If a boot source you
declare is too large to arrive whole, either shrink it, reorder `boot_path` so a
smaller entry passes surplus forward, or declare it under `boot_path_partial`
with a REASON — a bare path there is rejected, because an exemption with no
stated reason is a silencer rather than a decision.
